Fortenv is a defensive security library for Node.js applications.

It restricts ambient access to configured secrets in process.env, delivers selected secrets to explicitly registered functions, and reports denied access attempts through a dependency-free telemetry interface. Its implementation uses Node.js module loading, source transformation, proxies, and runtime integrity checks. These capabilities may resemble techniques that also appear in security research or malicious software.

Fortenv is intended to help application developers reduce the exposure of environment secrets to unrelated or compromised dependencies running in the same Node.js process. It does not provide a sandbox or an operating-system security boundary. Code can still obtain a secret when the application explicitly passes it, exposes an authorized wrapper, allows access before Fortenv starts, or exposes the startup environment through operating-system facilities such as Linux /proc/self/environ.

The source repository includes adversarial tests that exercise these boundaries. Those tests exist to validate defensive behavior and are not included in the published package.
